ot deployment|Contact|link|Mask' inputs/Q.tex inputs/P.tex\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"inputs/P.tex:16:\\\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}\\ninputs/P.tex:155:\\\\thanks{This work was supported by Carl Zeiss Foundation with the ReScaLe project, and by the BrainLinks-BrainTools center of the University of Freiburg. {Contact: \\\\tt\\\\footnotesize hartzj@cs.uni-freiburg.de}}%\\ninputs/P.tex:210:Long task horizons, high-dimensional action spaces, and stringent precision requirements typically demands large amounts of training data.\\ninputs/P.tex:248:    By estimating kinematic links from demonstrations, it distinguishes independently moving objects (\\\\textcolor{tabblue}{blue}) from those manipulated by the robot (\\\\textcolor{taborange}{orange}).\\ninputs/P.tex:283:The reference frames used by the policy are assumed to be determined by the environment and independent of the robot\u2019s own actions.\\ninputs/P.tex:292:The key idea is to infer kinematic links between the robot and task objects, which we detect directly from the policy streams.\\ninputs/P.tex:360:Additionally, as we detail in \\\\secref{sec:bench}, it maximizes task instance variability.\\ninputs/P.tex:432:For a Riemannian Gaussian with parameters \\\\(\\\\{\\\\boldsymbol\\\\mu_k, \\\\boldsymbol\\\\Sigma_k\\\\}\\\\), and \\\\(\\\\boldsymbol A_f\\\\) as the rotation matrix for \\\\(\\\\boldsymbol q_f\\\\), this transformation is given by\\\\looseness=-1\\ninputs/P.tex:484:The product-of-experts in \\\\eqref{eq:comb} weights the streams by their precision.\\ninputs/P.tex:486:As streams tied to irrelevant background clutter exhibit low precision, we can filter them out~\\\\cite{von2024art}.\\ninputs/P.tex:488:In our experiments, we use MiDiGaP~\\\\cite{von2025unreasonable}, a state-of-the-art Gaussian policy learning method, and pair it with TAPAS~\\\\cite{von2024art} to automatically segment skills and discard irrelevant task parameters based on their relative precision.\\ninputs/P.tex:505:    \\\\caption{Kinematic link detection.\\ninputs/P.tex:512:    The right y-axes show the precision determinants, where \\\\textcolor{tabblue}{blue} denotes no kinematic link between object and robot, and \\\\textcolor{taborange}{orange} denotes a link.\\ninputs/P.tex:516:    \\\\panelref{subfig:begin}: initially, neither mug nor tree is linked to the robot. \\ninputs/P.tex:521:    \\\\panelref{subfig:grasped}: after grasping, the mug becomes kinematically linked and follows the end-effector (\\\\textcolor{taborange}{$\\\\rcurvearrowright$}).\\ninputs/P.tex:522:    \\\\panelref{subfig:placed}: the link ends after placement.\\ninputs/P.tex:526:    Short-lived precision peaks at pre-grasp and during mug\u2013tree alignment do not constitute kinematic links.\\ninputs/P.tex:546:As the product-of-experts in \\\\eqref{eq:comb} weights the streams by their precision, this single stream then dominates the joint model.\\ninputs/P.tex:553:We resolve this conundrum by dynamically filtering out frames the moment they become kinematically linked to the end-effector, thereby restoring the exogeneity of the task parameters.\\ninputs/P.tex:561:Within each skill, we identify kinematic links using the stream precisions.\\ninputs/P.tex:562:Since TAPAS already pre-computes these precisions, this introduces minimal computational overhead.\\ninputs/P.tex:574:    After grasping, the mug is kinematically linked with the end-effector and \\\\ourmethod{} masks it out to prevent model collapse.\\ninputs/P.tex:590:    The Mug frame is used initially, but is ignored once kinematically linked.\\ninputs/P.tex:598:Let \\\\(\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\Lambda}_{t}^{(f)} = (\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\Sigma}_{t}^{(f)})^{-1}\\\\) be the precision matrix of stream \\\\(f\\\\) at time \\\\(t\\\\).\\ninputs/P.tex:601:While sustained spikes in its determinant  \\\\(\\\\det(\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\Lambda}_{t}^{(f)})\\\\) indicate a kinematic link, this raw magnitude is highly sensitive to regularization and difficult to threshold intuitively.\\ninputs/P.tex:602:Instead, we identify links using the Geometric Mean Standard Deviation:\\ninputs/P.tex:603:\\\\begin{equation}\\\\label{eq:gmsd}\\ninputs/P.tex:604:    M_t^{(f)} = \\\\left\\\\lvert \\\\det\\\\left(\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\Lambda}_{t}^{(f)}\\\\right)\\\\right\\\\rvert^{-\\\\frac{1}{2d}},\\ninputs/P.tex:609:If \\\\(M_t^{(f)} < \\\\tau_M\\\\), the end-effector and frame \\\\(f\\\\) are kinematically linked at time \\\\(t\\\\).\\ninputs/P.tex:611:Alternatively, the weighted precision \\\\(\\\\boldsymbol{W}\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\Lambda}_t^{(f)}\\\\boldsymbol{W}\\\\) with diagonal weight matrix \\\\(\\\\boldsymbol{W}\\\\) offers more fine-grained control over position and rotation.\\ninputs/P.tex:612:In our experiments, kinematic links were sufficiently apparent even without weighting.%\\ninputs/P.tex:620:\\\\footnote{Highly precise pre-grasps can occasionally cause brief, false-positive precision spikes (\\\\figref{fig:kinematic_analysis}). These artifacts can be safely ignored or filtered via temporal variance. Because this phase of the trajectory is fully determined by the pre-grasp pose, temporarily dropping the object frame introduces no spatial ambiguity.\\ninputs/P.tex:625:Our kinematic link analysis and TAPAS' task-parameter selection are grounded in Information Theory, a theoretical connection that has thus far been missing from the literature.\\ninputs/P.tex:626:For Gaussian policies, the conditional entropy $H(\\\\pose_\\\\ee \\\\ |\\\\ f)$ is proportional to $\\\\log\\\\det(\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\Sigma}_{t}^{(f)})$. \\ninputs/P.tex:627:Consequently, the mutual information $I(\\\\pose_\\\\ee ; f) = H(\\\\pose_\\\\ee) - H(\\\\pose_\\\\ee \\\\mid f)$ scales proportionally to $-\\\\log\\\\det(\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\Sigma}_{t}^{(f)})$. \\ninputs/P.tex:629:From this perspective, TAPAS selects parameters that maximize $I(\\\\pose_\\\\ee ; f)$, and \\\\ourmethod{} isolates the pathological case where a kinematic link drives spatial variance to zero, causing $I