primary tutorial, Section 4, https://calinon.ch/papers/Calinon-JIST2015.pdf : task-parameterized Gaussian models already use covariance eigenspaces and subspace methods. The note now cites this. Thus generic eigenvalue inspection has no stand-alone novelty. The surviving question is whether action-conditioned frame-role assignment matters empirically at Q\\\\x27s changing contacts, with P\\\\x27s binary mask as baseline.\\n#24 [emmy/finding] Best account before ready: I sharpened the assigned-pair question from direct P-on-Q transfer to causal role and partial-constraint selection for object pose. P's dynamic multi-stream policy treats low-variance object-relative end-effector pose as a kinematic link and masks the whole object frame (inputs/P.tex, Multi-Stream Policy Learning; Manipulation in Dynamic Environments, Eq. gmsd). Q's tendon hand rotates a grasped cube about one axis with seven motor channels and only encoder input to its deployed policy (inputs/Q.tex, Hand Design; Learning In-Hand Cube Rotation; Zero-shot deployment). A covariance with five narrow directions and free yaw has \\\\(M=(\\\\epsilon^5s)^{1/6}\\\\), so P's scalar can signal a full link despite free yaw; observational covariance also cannot tell object-causes-robot from robot-causes-object (ada/causal_frame_note.md). Q's calibrated simulator makes matched motor interventions and contact labels possible. The first decisive result would measure P gate errors against object response to randomized motor perturbations; a stronger publication needs a matched seven-motor controller that uses endogenous cube yaw as feedback, versus P-style exogenous frame guidance. Neither Q nor P supplies that adapter, Q has no logged physical cube pose, and simulation contact fidelity is unproved. Calinon already treats covariance subspaces (https://calinon.ch/papers/Calinon-JIST2015.pdf), and visual in-hand feedback already exists (https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.11744), so the pair supports a falsifiable path, not a novelty or performance claim. Longer derivation: emmy/causal-frame-test.md.\\n#25 [ada/correction] Formatting correction to #21 and #23: the raw sequence backslash-x-2-7 denotes an apostrophe and has no mathematical meaning. Read the affected phrases as the threshold reported by P and the weighting suggested by P. All substantive equations in #21 remain as written.\\n#26 [emmy/ready] ready at #24\\nlatest substantive entry: 25\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_33\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The two lines now fit together: observational precision can misidentify causal direction, and its determinant can hide a free pose direction. The publishable test is whether those diagnostic errors affect a matched seven-motor controller. The current papers do not establish that outcome.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_34\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 26'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_34\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada read --since 26'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#27 [emmy/ready] ready at #25\\nlatest substantive entry: 25\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_35\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada ready --seen 27'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_35\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor ada ready --seen 27'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"28\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_36\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I sharpened the question from \u201ccan P run on Q\u2019s hand?\u201d to **whether P\u2019s object-frame gate assigns the right role to an object during changing contact**. P masks an object frame when its pose stream has low geometric mean spread ([P, lines 597\u2013612](/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q8P8/inputs/P.tex:597)). That statistic cannot determine whether the robot drives the object or tracks it; it can also mask a frame with substantial yaw variation when its other pose directions are tightly constrained. Q supplies a calibrated seven-motor hand and cube-rotation simulator in which motor perturbations could test those distinctions ([Q, lines 644\u2013669](/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q8P8/inputs/Q.tex:644)). The derivation and proposed test are in [my note](/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q8P8/ada/causal_frame_note.md).\\n\\nThis is a **falsifiable publication path, not an established gain**. First measure frame-gate errors against cube responses to matched motor interventions. A stronger result would require a seven-motor controller comparison using tracked cube yaw as feedback; Q\u2019s deployed policy has no physical cube-pose log ([Q, lines 773\u2013784](/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q8P8/inputs/Q.tex:773)). Generic covariance-subspace analysis is already known ([Calinon\u2019s tutorial](https://calinon.ch/papers/Calinon-JIST2015.pdf)). I marked the ledger ready at entry #28. Unresolved are measured gate failures, the controller adapter, physical pose tracking, and control benefit.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"tu